You&rsquo;ve tried ChatGPT. You typed &ldquo;help me with my business&rdquo; and got a wall of generic nonsense that could apply to literally any company on Earth.
The problem isn&rsquo;t AI. The problem is your prompts.
Here are 10 prompts that actually work — each one tested and refined for small business owners who need real, actionable results.
1. The Weekly Content Calendar Generator Most business owners know they need to post on social media. Few know what to post. This prompt solves that:
&ldquo;Create a 7-day social media content calendar for my [type of business]. Include post ideas for Instagram, Facebook, and LinkedIn. Focus on [goal: brand awareness/sales/engagement]. My target audience is [describe audience]. Include hashtag suggestions for each post.&rdquo;
Why it works: Instead of asking AI to &ldquo;help with social media,&rdquo; you&rsquo;re giving it your specific business type, goal, and audience. The output goes from generic to actionable.
2. The Sales Page Writer Writing sales copy is hard. Writing it with AI is easy — if you know how to ask:
&ldquo;Write sales page copy for [product/service] priced at $[price]. Include: a headline that hooks, problem agitation, solution reveal, 5 key benefits (not features), 3 testimonial placeholders, FAQ section (5 questions), and a strong CTA. Target audience: [describe].&rdquo;
Why it works: The structure forces AI to follow a proven sales page framework instead of producing a generic paragraph about your product.
3. The Email Welcome Sequence Your new subscribers are most engaged in the first week. This prompt creates a 5-email sequence that turns them into customers:
&ldquo;Create a 5-email welcome sequence for new subscribers to my [type of business]. Email 1: Welcome + free value. Email 2: My story/brand story. Email 3: Best content/resource. Email 4: Social proof/testimonials. Email 5: Soft pitch for [product/service]. Write full email copy for each.&rdquo;
Why it works: It maps out the entire customer journey from &ldquo;who are you?&rdquo; to &ldquo;take my money.&rdquo;
4. The Objection Handler Every business hears the same objections. This prompt prepares you for all of them:
&ldquo;List the top 10 objections customers might have about buying [product/service] at $[price]. For each objection, write a persuasive response that addresses the concern and redirects to value. Keep responses conversational.&rdquo;
Why it works: You&rsquo;ll never be caught off-guard in a sales conversation again.
5. The 90-Day Business Plan Big goals fail without weekly actions. This prompt breaks your ambitions into actual steps:
&ldquo;Create a 90-day action plan for my [type of business]. Current revenue: $[amount]. Goal: $[target]. Break it into: Month 1 (foundation), Month 2 (growth), Month 3 (scale). Include weekly milestones and specific action items.&rdquo;
Why it works: It forces specificity. Not &ldquo;grow revenue&rdquo; but &ldquo;do exactly this, this week.&rdquo;
6. The Competitor Analysis Know what your competitors are doing — and where they&rsquo;re vulnerable:
&ldquo;Analyze my top 3 competitors: [names/URLs]. For each, identify: their positioning, pricing strategy, strengths, weaknesses, content strategy, and customer sentiment. Then suggest 3 ways I can differentiate.&rdquo;
Why it works: AI can process competitor information faster than any human. Feed it their websites and get a strategic analysis in minutes.
7. The Standard Operating Procedure Writer If you can&rsquo;t hand a task off, your business can&rsquo;t scale. This prompt builds the documentation:
&ldquo;Write a detailed SOP for [specific process in your business]. Include: purpose, scope, step-by-step instructions, tools needed, quality checkpoints, and common mistakes to avoid. Format it so anyone could follow it.&rdquo;
Why it works: SOPs are the difference between &ldquo;I&rsquo;m the only one who can do this&rdquo; and &ldquo;anyone can do this while I focus on growth.&rdquo;
8. The Lead Magnet Creator Free content that attracts paying customers. This prompt generates ideas and helps you build them:
&ldquo;Suggest 10 lead magnet ideas for a [type of business] targeting [audience]. For each idea, include: the title, format (PDF, checklist, template, etc.), what problem it solves, and an estimated creation time.&rdquo;
Why it works: Instead of guessing what your audience wants, you get 10 validated ideas ranked by effort and impact.
9. The Customer Survey Builder Stop guessing what your customers think. Ask them:
&ldquo;Create a post-purchase survey (10 questions) for [product/service]. Mix of: satisfaction rating, open-ended feedback, NPS score, improvement suggestions, and testimonial request. Keep it under 5 minutes to complete.&rdquo;
Why it works: It balances quantitative data (ratings) with qualitative insights (open-ended questions) and sneaks in a testimonial request.
10. The Revenue Stream Brainstorm You&rsquo;re probably leaving money on the table. This prompt finds it:
&ldquo;I run a [type of business]. Current revenue streams: [list]. Brainstorm 10 additional revenue streams I could add. For each: description, startup cost estimate, time to first revenue, and revenue potential (low/medium/high).&rdquo;
Why it works: Most business owners are too close to their business to see adjacent opportunities. AI isn&rsquo;t.
